  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 317 PM CDT Sun Oct 4 2026\n\nTemperatures exceeded expectations again today with widespread\ntemperatures in the mid to upper 70s as of 3pm. Winds remain\nlight through this evening and tonight. Overnight lows will once\nagain dip into the 40s in many locations, and this looks to be\nthe coolest night we will see over the upcoming week. \n\nAfter a relatively cool start Monday, temperatures should once\nagain return to the 70s (possibly pushing 80 degrees in the\nwest). This warmup will be aided by increasing southerly winds.\nAfternoon gusts could exceed 30 MPH in northern and western\nportions of the area. This southerly flow will also advect\nhigher dewpoints into the area, therefore overnight lows are\nonly expected to dip into the 50s through the rest of the work\nweek. \n\nTuesday looks to be the warmest day of the week, and this will\nfeel especially true as winds decrease with the arrival of a\nsurface trough. Raw model output (and the NBM) has been \nstruggling with temperatures in this transition. In general, \nthe \"old school\" MOS guidance has been more reliable as of late,\nand it is suggesting widespread high temperatures in the 80s\nTuesday. This isn't record- breaking territory, but it is still\na solid 10+ degrees above normal for this time of year. \n\nA weak cold front on Wednesday/Thursday will knock down\ntemperatures closer to normal for the end of the week. Ensembles\nhint at a low rain potential in the Thursday timeframe as well,\nbut probabilities on the latest NBM remain below 10%. There is\nmore ensemble support for rain in the Sunday night/Monday\ntimeframe, although this obviously remains very uncertain at\nthis range.",
